er  of pieces every second week we'll post a  pset and i'll give you about two weeks  to respond to that pset more on how we  evaluate in this class  in a different recording um just to say  here already that  there is going to be two all exams uh  towards the middle of the  semester and towards the end um very  short  uh little test 15 minutes per student i  will set up individual  meetings to um to go over this  um and then there's friday as an office  hour i haven't specified the time for  this yet  i'll send you a doodle poll and we'll  find the time which works for most of  you  on a friday friday afternoon or friday  morning  um this class doesn't have a final  so it basically ends with the last day  of class which is  um with this oral exams on in the week  in the 15th week's  15th week of this class  looking a little bit into the content um  you know the first week is really on  introduction and some historic remarks  we talk about  a relativistic kinematics and then we go  along  uh the outline as given in one of our  textbooks um  again textbooks are being discussed yet  in a different  short presentation but i'll really  follow along this textbook  but not stick too much to  you know only the content of that  textbook and i will supplement the  information and  using other textbooks as well  specifically when it comes to  some of the problems um and discussions  which come along  you can see that we'll start with  particle physics uh talk about quark  leptons and interactions  we'll talk about in how this fits in a  theoretical  way with invariance principle and  conversation laws and we'll look at  scattering and then qcd  before we go into weak interaction and  from there on we built the standard  model  with the electro weak interaction the  hex mechanism  so week 8 then or the end of week it  will give us a break  where we then start talking about  nuclear physics  you know this order of talking about  particle physics and then nuclear  physics  makes a lot of sense to me um it doesn't  make sense in historic in the  torah context because nuclear physics  ideas were developed before  the standard model for example was  developed but in order to fully  appreciate nuclear physics it's useful  to have introduced  basic interactions and therefore this is  the reason for this  for this order and then we try to bring  in  experimental methods with accelerators  colliding beams  um and experimental methods on how  particles interact with matter and  detectors  um again in in this in this uh schedule  thanksgiving thanksgiving week is off  and we'll use  the last remaining week to discuss  physics beyond the standard model and  the  connection to cosmology i said in the  title um the schedule will be tuned  i very much  expect that we have a little bit  slippage in this but there is some room  at the end to make sure that we don't  overrun  and that i don't give you too much to  learn to reach to watch movies  or you know do presets as we go week per  week  this is it for this first recording um  you know as always you can reach out and  ask any any sort of question either  during the recitation  office hours or separate emails

